WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:06.180
John Wick: Chapter 3, Parabellum takes audiences
even further into a world of assassins, intrigue,

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.480
and headshot after headshot.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:10.220
While reviews have been strong, it's not without
its flaws.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:13.680
Let's break down the highlights and lowlights
of John Wick: Chapter 3.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.980
Right from the start, Chapter 3 feels different
than its predecessors, largely thanks to the

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:20.860
inclusion of a ticking clock element.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:24.380
While John was never particularly leisurely
about conducting business during the previous

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:27.660
two movies, he did usually feel like he had
some room to breathe.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.920
But any sense of leisure is completely thrown
out the window in Parabellum, which opens

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.280
with a hero who is wounded, weary, and on
the run.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.239
Watching a man with his back against the wall
makes the audience root extra hard for him,

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.860
as ever-more-dire circumstances tax his wits
and will to survive.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.860
"Tick tock Mr. Wick."

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.160
Even after the man-on-the-run plot gets resolved,
the movie still ends on a tense note with

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:52.809
a thrilling siege on the previously impregnable
Continental, putting the good guys in a defensive

00:00:52.809 --> 00:00:55.620
position as the High Tables hits them with
everything they've got.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.360
"Would you help set the mood for our new guests?"

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:00.020
"Let us begin."

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.580
Previous antagonists for John Wick have all
been very impressive.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:07.720
While the particulars of each character differed,
they all felt like peers to John, sharing

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.300
a sense of professional rivalry, courtesy,
and mutual respect.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:15.659
They were calm, cool, and had their own agendas,
often feeling like they were starring in their

00:01:15.659 --> 00:01:19.420
own, equally interesting narratives running
parallel to John's, which really helped sell

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:22.500
the idea of this sprawling world of workmanlike
assassins.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.460
"You working?"

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:27.720
"Yeah.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.440
You?

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.440
"Yeah."

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:36.020
But despite embodying a sense of calmness,
coolness, and worldbuilding, the third movie's

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:38.560
Adjudicator character is a jarring presence.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.160
Working on behalf of the High Table, her scenes
have a tendency to grind the movie to a halt

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.920
as she plays detective to events we've already
seen unfold.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:51.200
Unlike other supporting cast members like
Winston, Charon, or the Bowery King, the Adjudicator

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:53.500
has a serious deficit of character and charm.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.180
The only reason she exists is to serve as
a threat to John Wick's allies, giving the

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.720
people who we know like John a reason to continue
doing the right thing by him while he's on

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:01.720
the run.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:06.020
She feels like a walking plot device, basically
because that's all she is, and compared to

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:09.400
previous villains in the series, she's a serious
step down.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.440
If there's one thing you absolutely cannot
count as a knock against John Wick 3, it's

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:18.320
the action sequences, which are hands-down
the best and most creative setpieces in the

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:19.680
entire series.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.900
Even more than the previous movies, Parabellum
is a fight scene delivery system, and we mean

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:25.020
that as a compliment.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.740
What keeps the wall-to-wall action from ever
getting dull, at least for the most part,

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.980
is the sheer variety going on in these kill-or-be-killed
conflicts.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.080
Are you bored with pistol-based gun-fu?

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.980
Well, how about everyone just throws knives
at each other for awhile?

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.980
Do you want to see a horse used as a weapon,
and then a getaway vehicle?

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:41.620
How about a motorcycle chase?

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:44.870
And are you at all curious about what might
happen if we combined the horse action with

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:45.960
the motorcycle chase?

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.440
The results may surprise you, and they'll
definitely keep you engaged.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.340
There is one exception to the otherwise sterling
action scenes in Parabellum, and that's the

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.680
movie's weird number of fight sequences that
don't involve John Wick.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.500
Parabellum cuts away to see what the villains
are up to with some frequency, following a

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:05.500
team of basically ninjas working on behalf
of the Adjudicator doing battle with John

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:06.500
Wick's allies.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.180
These scenes, which are essentially henchmen
vs. henchmen, occasionally get kind of boring,

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.860
since the audience just isn't invested in
the survival of any one combatant.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:19.480
None of these scenes invite the same enthusiasm
as a fight featuring John Wick, the only person

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.840
who we all really want to see get out of the
movie alive.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.900
While the stunt work in these scenes is still
top-notch, they leave the viewer with a slightly

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:30.640
disengaged feeling, not caring about the outcome,
like watching a video game play itself.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:35.160
The fights that don't involve John Wick may
be the weakest parts of Parabellum, but there

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.760
is one big exception, Halle Berry's Sofia.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.080
The put-upon, world-weary dog lover from John's
shadowy past is given plenty of room to be

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.340
established as capable and interesting.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:48.530
Her presence allows for not only the series'
first co-op fight, but also an opportunity

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.740
to get her incredible dogs in on the action.

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:54.640
Not only do the dogfight moments bring the
series full-circle, at least when it comes

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:58.560
to the puppy thing, it's also just a rare
treat to watch trained dogs tear into stunt

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.860
performers with expert precision while human
combatants continue to fight around them.

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:07.220
It's interspecies choreography of a rarely-seen
sort, probably because it looks really difficult

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:08.220
to execute.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:12.800
Rather than stealing the spotlight, or feeling
shoehorned in, Sofia holds her own as a totally

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:14.260
believable equal to John.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:16.360
She also doesn't overstay her welcome.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:20.819
She greets our hero by shooting him, gripes
at him, helps him kill, like, fifty people,

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:24.100
and leaves him with one final insult before
dipping out of the movie, never to be seen

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:25.080
again.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.140
It somehow all comes off as charming, and
leaves you wanting more.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:30.240
"Nice suit."

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:32.720
"Good to see you, too."

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:37.560
The first John Wick has one tight screenplay,
keeping things simple while hinting at a tantalizing

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:39.300
world just beneath the surface.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.610
The second movie further established the rules
and the history of this weird, wild underground

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:47.099
in a way that occasionally veered into the
realm of the ludicrous; by the end of the

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:50.900
second movie, it's fair to wonder if there's
anybody in New York City who isn't an international

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:51.900
hitman.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:55.900
But all of this gets significantly escalated
in Chapter 3, which expands the world to the

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:57.260
point of absurdity.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:01.240
This no longer feels like a heightened but
theoretically possible world, it feels like

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:03.460
the Hogwarts School of Karate and Bloodshed.

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:08.979
There's an ever-expanding compendium of significant
coins and tokens, a clandestine dance academy-slash-wrestling

00:05:08.979 --> 00:05:13.360
school run by a crime lord, and obscure rituals
of branding and finger-chopping that land

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:14.860
with an element of randomness.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:18.860
At times, the plot turns are so out-of-step
with anything resembling reality that they

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:23.680
become downright surreal, such as John's days-long
aimless walk through an endless desert.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:27.860
It's all just too much, and you wonder just
how much more exotic things can really become.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:31.400
It gets to the point where if the next movie
sees John Wick having to go to the literal

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:34.720
Moon for some reason, you feel like you wouldn't
be all that surprised.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:39.780
From start to finish, John Wick 3 serves up
beautifully choreographed, crowd-pleasing,

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:44.100
honestly just insane fights that are clear,
easy-to-follow, and immensely satisfying to

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:45.200
watch unfold.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:48.960
It's incredible to see what appears to be
accomplished in-camera in this movie.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.880
Where lesser action films would disguise fight
sequences with hundreds of cuts and rapid

00:05:52.889 --> 00:05:57.150
editing, Parabellum lets things flow, with
stunts proceeding in single shots as though

00:05:57.150 --> 00:06:00.029
to signal to the viewer that "yeah, we really
did that."

00:06:00.029 --> 00:06:04.050
Despite how stylized the movie is, all of
the fights feel real, and brutal, whether

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:08.229
you're watching John Wick insert the sharp
part of a knife into the soft part of an eyeball,

00:06:08.229 --> 00:06:10.389
or Sofia's dogs wreck groin after groin.

00:06:10.389 --> 00:06:11.900
It's just awesome to behold.

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:16.240
There's a reason why Parabellum has that ballet
motif, more than ever, these action scenes

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:19.920
come off like precise performances of a violent,
dangerous dance.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:23.700
The concept of Parabellum is simple: John
Wick is on the run.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:27.699
It's a solid hook to hang a story on, and
the idea of John Wick having to escape from

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.980
New York while all of New York tries to kill
him is irresistible.

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:35.200
So it's weird when this compelling plot gets
wrapped up in pretty anticlimactic fashion.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:39.260
After trudging his way through the desert
until he collapses, John Wick is found and

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:43.140
transported, as if by magic, to a summit with
an elder of the High Table.

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:46.900
His hope is to negotiate, in order to nullify
the bounty put on his life.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:50.960
Shockingly, this is all granted without a
lot of argument; all John has to do is slice

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:55.419
his ring finger off, give up some jewelry,
and promise to kill Winston, which he totally

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:57.480
doesn't even kind of plan on doing.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:02.000
It's hard to tell exactly when the John Wick
movies became the sexiest series in action

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:02.880
filmmaking.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:07.319
The first one followed our grieving hero as
he smashed, stabbed, and shot his way through

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:08.960
a series of somewhat grounded locales.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.000
The locations were all richly-decorated, but
they also weren't anything we haven't seen

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:17.420
before, building up to a quick and dirty showdown
at the ugly, rain-drenched docks.

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:21.340
But something got switched up in the second
movie, which increasingly began portraying

00:07:21.349 --> 00:07:26.240
John Wick's world as a super-stylish place
of infinite mirrors, trippy lights, and 4K

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:28.280
Ultra HD Winamp visualizers.

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:31.700
That's a trend that continues in Parabellum,
to the movie's benefit.

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:37.440
From the neon pink aesthetics of the NYC exteriors
to the Continental's secret acid trip chamber,

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:41.039
John Wick 3 is a movie where every location
is designed to impress, whether they come

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:42.720
off as realistic or not.

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:45.960
The John Wick movies have never been completely
realistic.

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:49.220
But the train really leaves the station on
that front in Chapter 3.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:53.419
The most egregious example of the movie getting
weird with its realism is the character of

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:57.120
Zero, an utterly goony clown of a villain
who feels like he walked in from a different

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:58.080
movie.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.500
Why can't this guy take a single fight seriously?

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:04.540
Why is he still yukking it up with a sword
sticking out of his body, even as he teeters

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:05.960
on the precipice of death?

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:10.819
In a weird way, Zero and his comrades almost
act as audience surrogates, frequently in

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:13.040
awe of just how much of a badass John Wick
is.

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:15.780
"And so far, you haven't disappointed!"

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:17.940
But where did this phenomenon come from?

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:21.400
No one in the last two movies spent this much
time fangirling it up about how cool John

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:22.400
is.

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:23.700
They were all business.

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:27.460
Sure, they regarded Wick as a figure to be
respected and feared, but when it came time

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:29.840
to be at odds with him, they fought him like
a peer.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:35.040
"Gin, wasn't it?"

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:39.980
The dorks John fights in the climax of Parabellum
don't convey that sort of vibe at all.

00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:44.440
We know the world of John Wick is larger-than-life,
but until Parabellum, the characters in that

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:46.040
world took things pretty seriously.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:50.180
Now, at least some seem very aware of the
fact that they're in an action movie, and

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:53.530
it makes for a fourth-wall breaking vibe that
doesn't necessarily work.

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:58.020
It may be a sign that this crazy series is
on the verge of getting a little too crazy.

00:08:58.260 --> 00:08:59.640
"I'll catch up with you, John!"

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:00.860
"No you won't."

